@page "/Projects/SiepieAndTakkieOriginal"
<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/SiepieAndTakkieOriginal" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
    <h3>Project description</h3>
        
    <span>
      My favourite project by far. The responsibility I carried and the game we made. Loved it.
      This project was between Swedish and Dutch students. It started with the direction we were given. The game must be about Stockholm and Amsterdam and if possible collaboration between them.
    
    </span>
    </div>
    <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Ben1HHvRK5o?si=l8tYg1qqdCP5MSKQ" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
    <StickyNote Text="Demo" OpenTo="StickyNote.TargetType.BlankPage" Link="https://37214.hosts2.ma-cloud.nl/Siepie" />
    <h3>
      I really wanted to show my best side
    </h3>
    <span>

      Not knowing these people seemed like the hardest part of this collaboration. So I focussed on making that my highest priority. I knew from previous research that pull requests were a good way to keep track of the code. So briefed my team on how to use them and after that they made a test pull request to see it for themselves. Thus we implemented them and I made sure that every pull request was checked and read. I also tried giving feedback on code where I felt it was helpful.

    </span>
    <h3>
      Agile workflow

    </h3>
    <span>
      I had only read some theory about working agile. But I knew the purpose of standups and standdowns. Thus since we had a very small time window (two weeks), I made sure we made use of them. Checking up on everyone every morning. Giving feedback if they finished their work the previous day and planning in the work for the coming day. Every standdown was more aimed at what work was finished and just chatting a little bit with eachother. I really enjoyed this workflow as it was very clear what everyone was doing all the time. Tackling bottlenecks quickly and efficiently whilst also having a bit fun.

    </span>
    <h3>
      The code I worked on during the period
    </h3>
    <span>
      I myself was tasked with writing the dialog system. As I was never giving true freedom with what I was making however I grossly overestimated the scope of my feature. I made a way too broad system with nothing 100 percent finished. But I learnt from that mistake and finished the feature after the initial project. This time it was less big but with unit test covering the whole thing.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>
